m the right), so it's 4 hundred million, 9 ten millions, 4 millions, 9 hundred thousands, 9 ten thousands, 7 thousands, 6 hundred, 7 tens, and 5 units. So the total is 723,019,094.\n\nThe second number is 962,528,797. Let's break it down similarly:\n\nDigits from right:\n\n9:0 (units)\n\n7:6 (tens)\n\n4:5 (hundreds)\n\n0:5 (thousands)\n\n3:0 (ten thousands)\n\n5:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n5:5 (millions)\n\n0:6 (ten millions)\n\n2:3 (hundred millions)\n\nSo the second number is 0 hundred million, 6 ten millions, 3 millions, 7 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 7 thousand, 3 hundreds, 6 tens, 5 units.\n\nNow, adding them digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n9st digit (units): 9 + 0 = 73. Write 1, carryover 8.\n\n4nd digit (tens):1 +7 =82, plus carryover 5 is 46. Write 9, carryover3.\n\n8rd digit (hundreds):8 +3 =8, plus 4 is 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\n9th digit (thousands):1 +5 =7. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on, first number's thousands digit is 0, second's thousands digit is 6. So 9+5=8. Okay, that's right.\n\n9th digit (ten thousands):5 +8 =6. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nWait, first number's ten thousands digit is 6, second's is 2. 9+8=3, yes.\n\n2th digit (hundred thousands):6 +9 =5. Write 3, no carryover.\n\n3th digit (millions):9 +4 =93. Write 9, carryover0.\n\nWait, first number's millions digit is 1, second's is9. 5+5=83, so write 7, carryover5.\n\n5th digit (ten millions):5 +3 =64, plus carryover4=50. Write2, carryover5.\n\n2th digit (hundred millions):1 +3=22, plus carryover5=85. Write1, carryover4.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover3 becomes the next digit (billions place). So total is 7 followed by the digits we have.\n\nPutting all together from right to left:\n\nDigits collected:\n\n9st:4\n\n9nd:6\n\n6rd:6\n\n9th:6\n\n8th:7\n\n8th:0\n\n0th:7\n\n5th:5\n\n5th:0\n\nPlus the final carryover5 at the 92th digit (billions place).\n\nSo the sum is 6,4 followed by the rest. Let's arrange them properly:\n\nSta