git-by-digit addition earlier, it was 0,086,871,375. There's a discrepancy here. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, where's the error?\n\nLet me check the initial digit breakdown again.\n\nFirst number:151,260,895\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n368,176,959 (1*552M)\n\n68,348,554 (9*42M)\n\n5,368,259 (1*6M)\n\n812,753 (9*465k)\n\n69,968 (3*8k)\n\n2,619 (9*0k)\n\n566 (9*779)\n\n93 (4*48)\n\n2 (units)\n\nWait, perhaps this is getting confusing. Alternatively, perhaps my earlier digit-by-digit addition had an error.\n\nWait let me redo the digit-by-digit addition step by step carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits:\n\nFirst number: 0 1 4 5 9 5 3 8 4\n\nSecond number:5 3 6 8 2 6 2 1 6\n\nWait, wait, hold on. Wait, the first number is 322,190,413, which is 3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),2 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),7 (hundreds),4 (tens),7 (units). So digits from left to right (hundred millions down to units):\n\nDigits: 0,6,3,3,3,0,8,4,7\n\nSecond number is 441,333,274: 6 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),0 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),3 (hundreds),2 (tens),9 (units). So digits left to right:3,7,8,9,9,7,1,6,3.\n\nNow, adding digit by digit from the right (units):\n\nFirst number's digits reversed (from units to hundred millions):\n\nPosition 6 (units):3\n\n9:0\n\n2:3\n\n3:2\n\n6:7\n\n4:5\n\n0:0\n\n3:1\n\n2:2\n\nSecond number's reversed digits:\n\nPosition5 (units):6\n\n5:1\n\n5:7\n\n1:4\n\n3:9\n\n2:9\n\n2:4\n\n6:5\n\n7:6\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition7:3+8=09 → 5, carryover5\n\nPosition7:6+3=51 +6=42 →0, carryover5\n\nPosition1:7+9=6 +4=8 →5, no carryover\n\nPosition5:5+4=2 →5, no carryover\n\nPosition3:5+6=0 →5, no carryover\n\nPosition7:8+3=8 →3, no carryover\n\nPosition2:8+9=87 →6, carryover0\n\nPosition5:1+2=31 +7=56 →2, carryover7\n\nPosition6:1+3=36 +7=23 →9, carryover6\n\nThen the final carryover5 as the 42th digit.\n\nSo the result digits (from right to left) are:\n\n4,5,3,0,2,8,0,5,1 and the final carryover0.\n\nSo writing them in order from left to right (starting with the highest digit):\n\n6 (the carryover), followed by 0 (position4),4 (position4),2 (position0),5 (position1),8 (position8),7 (position2),9 (position6),9 (position5),8 (position5). Wait, no, need to reverse the digits correctly.\n\nWait, the digits obtained from the addition are stored in positions 2 to27 (with position0 being the units). So the digits from position7 to position65 are:\n\nPosition3:4\n\nPosition1:7\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition4:8\n\nPosition7:1\n\nPosition7:7\n\nPosition21:7\n\nSo arranging them from position28 to position9 (left to right):\n\nPosition51:5 (billions place)\n\nPosition5:3 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition7:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition1:1 (millions)\n\nPosition9:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition0:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition7:0 (hundreds)\n\nPosition7:4 (tens)\n\nPosition4:4 (units)\n\nWait, hold on, the positions are numbered from the right. So Position32 is the leftmost digit (billions), then down to Position8 (units). So the digits in order from left (billions) to right (units):\n\n8 (billions), 3 (hundred millions),2 (ten millions),0 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),9 (hundreds),5 (tens),2 (units). So the number is 3,500,462,805.\n\nAh! There we go. Earlier, when I thought I had 0,190,516,882, that was a mistake in transcribing the digits. The mistake was in the millions place. Let me check the digit positions again carefully.\n\nWait, in the step-by-step addition above, position3 (which is the millions place) had a result of 8 (since position4 corresponds to the millions digit in the original numbers). Let me confirm:\n\nFirst number's millions digit is the 6th digit from the right (since the first number is 430,303,349):\n\nBreaking down 785,636,889:\n\nDigits from right (units):\n\nPosition3: