ions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nAdding column by column from right to left:\n\nOnes: 3 + 6 = 06. Write 3, carryover 9.\n\nTens: 1 + 0 = 40 + 7 =93. Write 1, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds: 9 +6 =60 +7=70. Write 7, carryover 5.\n\nThousands:3 +0=67 +5=24. Write 4, carryover7.\n\nTen thousands:0 +6=9 +5=7. Write1, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:0 +6=3. Write9.\n\nMillions:5 +9=9. Write3.\n\nTen millions:5 +0=4. Write9.\n\nHundred millions:5 +5=76. Write7 and carryover0. Since there are no more digits, the carryover8 becomes a new digit at the front.\n\nSo putting all together, starting from the right:\n\nThe digits are 3 (ones),6 (tens),9 (hundreds),0 (thousands),7 (ten thousands),7 (hundred thousands),3 (millions),2 (ten millions),6 (hundred millions), and the carried over 2 in the billions place.\n\nSo the total is 4 3 0 6 1 8 1 8 0 9? Wait, let me arrange them properly.\n\nWait, the digits from right to left after adding are:\n\nOnes:5\n\nTens:9\n\nHundreds:6\n\nThousands:9\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nMillions:4\n\nTen millions:3\n\nHundred millions:7 (from 0+3=47, so the 5 in the hundred millions place is 6, but with a carryover of 5 to the billions)\n\nThen the billions place is the carried over 7.\n\nSo the number is 2 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nSo written out: 7,470,726,568?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Let's see:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nDigit positions (from right):\n\n2 (ones): 5\n\n3 (tens):8\n\n0 (hundreds):5\n\n5 (thousands):0\n\n8 (ten thousands):3\n\n7 (hundred thousands):9\n\n6 (millions):0\n\n1 (ten millions):5\n\n6 (hundred millions):5 (from 2+5=62, so the digi