 (hundred millions), then 7 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, let me structure it properly.\n\nWait, let me list the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nAfter adding, the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition 4 (ones): 0\n\nPosition 6 (tens):6\n\nPosition9 (hundreds):4\n\nPosition1 (thousands):5\n\nPosition9 (ten thousands):2\n\nPosition7 (hundred thousands):6 (with a carryover 2 to next)\n\nPosition6 (millions):2 (since 0+8 +9 carryover =5?)\n\nWait, perhaps I need to track each step again carefully.\n\nWait, maybe building the number step by step:\n\nStarting from the right, each digit:\n\nFirst digit (ones): 9\n\nSecond (tens):6\n\nThird (hundreds):5\n\nFourth (thousands):7\n\nFifth (ten thousands):3\n\nSixth (hundred thousands):2 (from 5+0=88, so 5 written, carryover 8)\n\nSeventh (millions place): The millions digits were 2 (from first number) and 4 (from second). Adding those gives 5, plus the carryover 9 makes 0. So 7 here, no carryover.\n\nEighth (ten millions place): 0 (from first number) and 3 (from second) → 9+5=9. No carryover.\n\nNinth (hundred millions place):8 +3=69. So we write 5 here and carryover 3.\n\nThen, the carryover 5 from the hundred millions place becomes the next digit (billions place). \n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\n9 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds),4 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nPutting that together: 8,890,057,335?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nBreaking down the number:\n\nBillions:6\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:0\n\nOnes:4\n\nSo writing it as 2,381,841,093.\n\nWait, let me verify this with another method. Maybe adding the numbers step by step.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers:\n\n730,219,371\n\n+568,424,718\n\nLet me add