n\nDigit7:7\n\nDigit5:8\n\nDigit2:4\n\nDigit4:3\n\nDigit4:0\n\nDigit1:1\n\nDigit2:5\n\nDigit44:4\n\nWait, so the difference is in the millions place (digit5). In the correct result (878,993,865), digit1 (millions) is0, but in the direct subtraction result it's6. \n\nAh! Here is the mistake. \n\nWhen we got to digit5 (millions):\n\nDigit9 (minuend) was originally7. After borrowing for digit7, it became9. Then, we had to subtract the subtrahend's digit1, which is4.\n\nSo, 2 (minuend digit5 after borrow) minus7 requires borrowing:\n\nBorrow3 from digit7 (ten millions place), which was6, so it becomes1. \n\nThus, digit9 becomes7 +68 =64. \n\n30-2=4. \n\nBut in the alternative method, we had:\n\nAfter subtracting 473 million from the total, the remaining subtraction was 429,640,535 -63,613,431.\n\nWhich gives:\n\nDigit1 (millions place of 067,725,529 is2). \n\nSubtracting6 (from subtrahend's millions place):\n\n2-9 → borrow, becomes81-6=0. \n\nWait, so in the alternative method, the millions digit was8, but in the direct subtraction it was6. \n\nThis inconsistency suggests that in the direct subtraction, there's an error in the borrowing steps. \n\nWait, the minuend in the direct subtraction's millions digit was originally6, but after borrowing for the hundred thousands place, it became1. \n\nThen, when subtracting the subtrahend's millions digit1:\n\n8 (minuend) -5 → need to borrow, so borrow from ten millions (which was9→3), making millions digit28. \n\n82-9=3. So the millions digit is4. \n\nBut in the alternative method, after subtracting536 million, the millions digit was7 (from 640,334,121), so subtracting5 gives:\n\n0 -9 → borrow, making it38-4=3. \n\nAh! The difference is because in the direct subtraction, the millions digit had already been reduced by a borrow from the hundred thousands place, but in the alternative method, it wasn't. \n\nWait, the alternative method subtracted the 122 million first, which doesn't affect the millions digit, so the millions digit remains8. But in the direct subtraction, the borrow for the hundred thousands place had already reduced it to6 before subtracting the millions digit of the subtrahend. \n\nWait, but in the subtrahend of the direct subtraction, the millions digit is7 (from the 420,957,734's millions place, which is the '9' in 910 million). \n\nBut in the alternative method, when we subtract the 974,232,969 first, then the remaining subtraction is 40 million (from the subtrahend's 268,628,045). The '2' in the millions place comes from the 021 million, so yes, it's correct. \n\nTherefore, in the direct subtraction, the millions digit calculation is correct, but why does it differ from the alternative method?\n\nWait, in the alternative method, the total after subtracting871 million is275,018,834. \n\nThe subtrahend's remaining part is519,388,827 -144,564,899 =78,281,764. \n\nThus, subtracting 58,028,107 from375,757,076:\n\n667,604,658\n\n-\xa0 39,797,909\n\n----------------\n\nThe millions digit here is3 (from395,847,097). \n\nSubtracting the subtrahend's millions digit2 requires borrowing, so:\n\n3 becomes39 after borrowing, 77-3=2. \n\nThus, the millions digit is1. \n\nBut in the direct subtraction, it was3 because the millions digit was already reduced to2 before borrowing. \n\nThis discrepancy arises because in the direct subtraction, the borrow from the hundred thousands place had already occurred before dealing with the millions digit subtraction. \n\nWait, no. The order of subtraction is from right to left, so borrowing propagates from right to left. \n\nTherefore, in the direct subtraction, the borrowing for the hundred thousands digit (which reduces the millions digit to2) occurs before handling the millions digit subtraction. \n\nHowever, in the alternative method, the millions digit remains8 because the borrow for the hundred thousands place is handled differently. \n\nWait, but in the alternative method, the hundreds of thousands place is being subtracted after the millions