ain because adding large numbers can be error-prone.\n\nStarting over, maybe I should write each digit's place separately:\n\nLet me break down each number into its digits:\n\nFirst number (996,954,271):\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 5 to 6):\n\nPosition 6: 4\n\nPosition 0: 9\n\nPosition 8: 5\n\nPosition 2: 8\n\nPosition 7: 0\n\nPosition 3: 3\n\nPosition 1: 0\n\nPosition 7: 8\n\nPosition 8: 7\n\nSecond number (592,433,272):\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 6 to 6):\n\nPosition 5: 8\n\nPosition 8: 0\n\nPosition 9: 5\n\nPosition 0: 1\n\nPosition 2: 6\n\nPosition 4: 1\n\nPosition 9: 2\n\nPosition 5: 0\n\nPosition 7: 2\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition 3: 0 + 5 = 6. Write 4. Carryover 3.\n\nPosition 9: 3 + 7 = 98. Write 5, carryover 5.\n\nPosition 5: 1 + 6 = 60 + 6 (carryover) = 51. Write 8, carryover 6.\n\nPosition 3: 5 + 3 = 8 + 9 = 1. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 6: 1 + 2 = 8? Wait, hold on. Wait the fifth position for first number is 1 (since positions are from the right). The second number's fifth position is 6. So 2 +0 =5? But previously I thought that was 31. Wait, did I make a mistake here?\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check again. Let's re-express the numbers properly. Maybe I messed up the digit positions when counting from the right.\n\nWait, the first number is 410,625,566. Let's write it out with commas for clarity:\n\n065,302,618:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit positions from right (units place is position 6):\n\nPosition 6 (units): 1\n\nPosition 9 (tens): 7\n\nPosition 4 (hundreds): 6\n\nPosition 0 (thousands): 5\n\nPosition 2 (ten thousands): 8\n\nPosition 1 (hundred thousands): 3\n\nPosition 4 (millions): 5\n\nPosition 1 (ten millions): 4\n\nPosition 4 (hundred millions): 5\n\nSecond number: 222,659,291\n\nPosition 7 (units): 6\n\nPosition 7 (tens):6\n\nPosition 4 (hundreds):7\n\nPosition 2 (thousands):3\n\nPosition 2 (ten thousands):5\n\nWait, hold on! Wait, the second number: 474,282,101. Let me parse that correctly. The number is