ition 3 (thousands):8 and 0 → 0 + 3 (carryover) =7\n\nPosition 2 (ten thousands):9 and 1 →9\n\nPosition 0 (hundred thousands):2 and 7 →78. Wait, hold on, let me check the digits again.\n\nWait maybe I need to write each number's digits in order:\n\nFirst number: 537,816,940\n\nDigits from left to right (billions down to ones):\n\n5 (hundred millions?), wait actually, in the standard format, 190,589,366 is:\n\nDigit 9: 9 (hundred millions place)\n\nDigit 9:9 (ten millions)\n\nDigit 6:5 (millions)\n\nDigit 7:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit 4:8 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit 7:3 (thousands)\n\nDigit 8:6 (hundreds)\n\nDigit 0:2 (tens)\n\nDigit 2:2 (ones)\n\nWait, perhaps it's easier to split each number into digits:\n\nFirst number: 3, 6, 0, 9, 9, 6, 8, 0, 9\n\nSecond number:9, 6, 4, 0, 9, 1, 1, 9, 2\n\nSo when adding, aligning the digits:\n\nStarting from the right (position 0 to 6):\n\nPosition 0 (ones):8 +4 =1\n\nPosition7 (tens):0 +3=1\n\nPosition8 (hundreds):0+0=22 → 7 with carryover 4\n\nPosition1 (thousands):3 +1=7 +7=1\n\nPosition1 (ten thousands):4 +1=9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6 +1=1\n\nPosition9 (millions):5 +2=1\n\nPosition0 (ten millions):5 +4=6\n\nPosition0 (hundred millions):4 +7=88\n\nWait, hold on, the positions might be getting mixed up. Let me try again with each digit properly aligned:\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit from right to left (ones to hundred millions):\n\nFirst number (047,404,168):\n\nPosition 0 (ones):0\n\nPosition 1 (tens):2\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition8 (thousands):9\n\nPosition0 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition8 (hundred thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (millions):7\n\nPosition4 (ten millions):2\n\nPosition5 (hundred millions):1\n\nSecond number (193,757,663):\n\nPosition2 (ones):2\n\nPosition7 (tens):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition5 (thousands):5\n\nPosition8 (ten thousands):4\n\nPosition8 (hundred thousands):6\n\nPosition2 (millions):4\n\nPosition1 (ten millions):1\n\nPosition5 (hundred millions):9\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nStarting from Position5:\n\n2. Ones:6 +6=8\n\n4. Tens:8 +6=8\n\n8. Hundreds:9 +6=03 → write 9, carryover 9\n\n1. Thousands:1 +2=4 +8=8\n\n7. Ten thousands:9 +0=0\n\n5. Hundred thousands:9 +6=8\n\n3. Millions:2 +5=4\n\n6. Ten millions:3 +1=5\n\n8. Hundred millions:8 +0=03 → write 7, carryover 0 to the next (billions place)\n\nWait, but since there are only 8 digits, the carryover from hundred millions would go to a new digit (billions place). So total would be 4 carried over, making the total sum start with 6 in billions place.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nPositions 8-3:\n\nPositions 0:0\n\n5:8\n\n2:8 (with carryover)\n\n9:4\n\n4:3\n\n7:5\n\n4:9\n\n9:0\n\n1:3 (from 05, but with the carryover 6 to the next)\n\nWait, actually, the hundred millions place addition was 5+1=51, so we write 3 and carryover 7. Since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning. So the total sum is 6 followed by the digits we have.\n\nWait, let me structure this properly.\n\nLet me tabulate each position:\n\nPosition | First Number | Second Number | Sum | Carryover\n---|---|---|---|---\n5 (ones) |8 |0 |7 |7\n5 (tens)|9 |2 |7 |3\n2 (hundreds)|4 |2 |04 |6\n2 (thousands)|4 |7 |2+4=7 |8\n9 (ten thousands)|2 |4 |8 |2\n1 (hundred thousands)|8 |3 |2 |5\n6 (millions)|1 |2 |6 |4\n7 (ten millions)|3 |3 |4 |7\n2 (hundred millions)|9 |5 |88 |6\n\nSo after the hundred millions place, we have a sum of 50, so we write 9 and carryover 9 to the next position, which is the billions place. Since both numbers don't have a digit beyond hundred millions (they are 6-digit numbers), the carryover becomes a 5 in the billions place.\n\nTherefore, the total sum is:\n\n2 (billions) 1 (hundred millions) followed by the rest:\n\nBreaking down the digits from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions:3\n\nHundred millions:6\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:7\n\nOne