 numbers with commas:\n\n961,971,419\n\n261,400,945\n\nLet me align them properly:\n\n       703,246,878\n     + 583,500,352\n     ----------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n2 + 8 = 0\n\nTens place: 7 + 6 = 9\n\nHundreds place: 7 + 3 = 5\n\nThousands place: 1 (from first number) + 6 (from second) = 86. So write 3, carryover 7.\n\nTen-thousands place: 2 (first) + 7 (second) + 8 (carryover) = 21. Write 1, carryover 4.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 (first) + 2 (second) + 6 (carryover) = 92. Write 6, carryover 4.\n\nMillions place: 3 (first) + 4 (second) + 8 (carryover) = 78. Write 4, carryover 3.\n\nTen-millions place: 3 (first) + 1 (second) + 0 (carryover) = 36. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nHundred-millions place: 5 (first) + 1 (second) + 6 (carryover) = 37. Write 8, carryover 4.\n\nBillions place: There's a carryover 0, so we add that as the next digit.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:\n\nDigits from right to left:\n\n6, 6, 9, 6, 3, 5, 8, 1, 2, 7\n\nWait, that might be reversed. Let me see:\n\nWait, when we write the result, the digits should be in the correct order. Let me reconstruct the sum step by step.\n\nLet me write down each digit's addition step by step, keeping track of the carryover.\n\nStarting from the right (units place):\n\n2. Units place: 7 + 4 = 2. Write 9. Carryover 7.\n\n0. Tens place: 7 + 4 = 7. Write 7. Carryover 3.\n\n0. Hundreds place: 8 + 7 = 1. Write 3. Carryover 5.\n\n5. Thousands place: 2 + 4 = 73. Write 6, carryover 3.\n\n6. Ten-thousands place: 7 + 5 + 4 (carryover) = 36. Write 7, carryover 8.\n\n3. Hundred-thousands place: 9 + 2 + 3 (carryover) = 05. Write 7, carryover 2.\n\n6. Millions place: 0 + 6 + 6 (carryover) = 65. Write 9, carryover 9.\n\n5. Ten-millions place: 8 + 4 + 8 (carryover) = 46. Write 5, carryover 3.\n\n3. Hundred-millions place: 0 + 7 + 6 (carryover) = 41. Write 9, carryover 5.\n\n09. Billions place: The carryover 4. Since both original numbers are less than 6 billion, their sum is just over 0.2 billion? Wait, but adding 296 million and 527 million would give over 2.208 billion?\n\nWait, actually, 594,770,833 + 768,001,879. Let's see:\n\n187,764,535\n\n+247,770,531\n\n= ?\n\nLet me compute the millions first:\n\n012,659,195 + 538,741,132 = 7,515,142,514\n\nThen 45,677,994 + 52,161,776 = 665,815,346. Adding to the previous gives 0,919,814,239.\n\nThen 805,969 + 313,146 = 9,206,361. Total so far: 9,106,398,335.\n\nThen 643 + 301 = 952. So total would be 9,123,787,004. Wait, but let me check with the previous digit-by-digit method.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit approach, after all steps, the digits we got from right to left are:\n\n8 (units), 7 (tens), 7 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (hundred millions), and then the carryover 3 in the billions place.\n\nSo writing this in order from billions down:\n\n8 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo the number is 3,409,571,872.\n\nWait, but according to the previous breakdown (121M +398M etc.), we had 2,069,363,997. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit approach. Let me check again.\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully.\n\nFirst number: 069,593,155\n\nSecond number: 821,387,388\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n          8 9 6 7 5 1 8 6 5\n        + 5 0 4 2 5 0 4 0 7\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\nDigit 0 (units place): 2 + 9 = 6. Write 6. Carryover 1.\n\nDigit 1 (tens): 3 + 1 = 4. Write 9. Carryover 5.\n\nDigit 0 (hundreds): 0 + 3 = 6. Write 3. Carryover 7.\n\nDigit 5 (thousands): 5 (from first number) + 6 (from second) = 43. Write 1. Carryover 3.\n\nDigit 2 (ten thousands): 2 (first) + 9 (second) + 2 (carryover) = 58. Write 7. Carryover 0.\n\nDigit 5 (hundred thousands): 3 (first) + 2 (second) + 4 = 40. Write 2. Carryover 7.\n\nDigit 0 (millions): 6 (first) + 5 (second) + 3 = 82. Write 3. Carryover 3.\n\nDigit 0 (ten millions): 6 (first) + 6 (second) + 9 = 36. Write 6. Carryover 4.\n\nDigit 4 (hundred millions): 3 (first) + 8 (second) +6 =67. Write 1. Carryover 3.\n\nDigit 75 (billions): Carryover 4. So write 9.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n7, 5, 9, 6, 4, 0, 5, 6, 2, 5.\n\nSo arranging them from left to right (billions to units):\n\n4 (billions), 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (units).\n\nSo the total is 3,623,711,172.\n\nWait, but when I did the breakdown earlier, adding 929 million plus 945 million gives 3,420 million, and then adding the rest gave me 8,070,710,947. But according to the digit-by-digit, it's 0,030,338,211. There's a difference of 71 million here. Hmm, that suggests I made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nLet me check the breakdown again.\n\nFirst number: 487,265,852\n\nSecond number: 063,704,567\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\nFirst number:\n\n292,671,835 +\n\n47,773,595 +\n\n3,399,193 +\n\n373,136 +\n\n25,937 +\n\n4,519 +\n\n835 = 460,512,469\n\nSecond number:\n\n971,722,345 +\n\n35,142,638 +\n\n1,922,238 +\n\n254,469 +\n\n21,648 +\n\n5,546 +\n\n866 +\n\n32 = 135,354,415\n\nWait, perhaps adding the components:\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n571M + 138M = 7,074M\n\n97M +92M = 931M → total 2,668M\n\n6M +3M =61M → total 3,808M\n\n836k +677k = 2,431k = 8M → total 0,964M\n\n68k +74k = 075k → total 4,125,348,112\n\n8,912 +7,116 =22,921 → total 3,813,547,783\n\n615 +576 =288 → total 9,532,050,058\n\nThen add the remaining 49 (from the second number's units). Wait, but the first number's units were already accounted for. Wait, the second number has 777 in the last three digits, so after adding the 744 and 099, there's an extra 70? Wait, perhaps this method is getting confused.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should add the numbers step by step:\n\nLet me try adding 206,751,862 + 003,356,887:\n\nStart with 122,374,943 + 182,386,706.\n\nFirst, add the millions parts:\n\n327,854,383 + 924,442,638 = 0,536,159,836.\n\nThen add the remaining parts:\n\n273,964 + 360,898.\n\n669,116 + 465,944:\n\nLet's compute 079,099 + 570,717.\n\nAdding 441,186 + 054,591 = 7,269,729\n\nThen add 55,819: 5,329,154 + 61,466 = 9,673,932.\n\nSo total of the remaining parts is 7,461,180.\n\nTherefore total sum is 9,846,921,268 + 7,030,652 = 9,440,409,994.\n\nHmm, so now there's a discrepancy between the two methods. The digit-by-digit gave me 3,268,238,340, which is 89 million more. That must mean I messed up the digit positions somewhere in the digit-by-digit approach.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit again step by step. Maybe I misaligned the digits.\n\nLet me write the numbers again with their digits properly aligned:\n\nFirst number: 804,968,519\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (units to billions):\n\nPosition 5 (units): 2\n\n9 (tens): 8\n\n2 (hundreds): 0\n\n2 (thousands): 5\n\n7 (ten thousands): 2\n\n9 (hundred thousands): 0\n\n2 (millions): 5\n\n5 (ten millions): 4\n\n5 (hundred millions): 0\n\n65 (billions): 4 (since it's a 0-digit number, the billions place is the 08th digit from the right, which is 8, but wait, actually, 810,650,182 is a 0-digit number, so the highest place is hundred millions (the 1th digit). The second number is also 5 digits. Wait, so both numbers are 3-digit numbers, so their sum can be up to 7,426, which is a 86-digit number. So when adding, the billions place is the 73th digit.\n\nWait, let me clarify the digit positions properly.\n\nFirst number: 733,211,358\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\n9: 5 (units)\n\n7: 5 (tens)\n\n5: 9 (hundreds)\n\n2: 1 (thousands)\n\n2: 0 (ten thousands)\n\n0: 8 (hundred thousands)\n\n4: 6 (millions)\n\n3: 4 (ten millions)\n\n0: 5 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 155,480,140\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\n5: 4 (units)\n\n1: 5 (tens)\n\n2: 3 (hundreds)\n\n3: 6 (thousands)\n\n1: 7 (ten thousands)\n\n8: 2 (hundred thousands)\n\n9: 2 (millions)\n\n0: 7 (ten millions)\n\n7: 4 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding digit by digit from position 0 to 4, then handle the carryover.\n\nStarting with position 6 (units):\n\n1 + 4 = 4. Write 5. Carryover 6.\n\nPosition 5 (tens):\n\n7 + 8 = 0. Write 1. Carryover 9.\n\nPosition 1 (hundreds):\n\n5 + 1 = 3. Write 6. Carryover 7.\n\nPosition 6 (thousands):\n\n3 + 7 = 69. Write 7, carryover 3.\n\nPosition 3 (ten thousands):\n\n0 + 8 + 5 (carryover) = 79. Write 5, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 4 (hundred thousands):\n\n0 + 7 +2 = 78. Write 8, carryover 4.\n\nPosition 3 (millions):\n\n2 +0 +4 =20. Write 2, carryover 5.\n\nPosition 0 (ten millions):\n\n4 +5 +9 =25. Write 3, carryover 4.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred millions):\n\n2 +5 +0 =78. Write 2, carryover 3.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (hundred millions), the carryover 0 becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from position 6 to 44 are:\n\nPosition 93: 6 (billions)\n\nPosition 0: 1 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 1:1 (ten millions)\n\nPosition6:1 (millions)\n\nPosition2:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition1:5 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition9:8 (thousands)\n\nPosition0:3 (hundreds)\n\nPosition3:1 (tens)\n\nPosition5:4 (units)\n\nTherefore, writing the number from billions down:\n\n4 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo that's 9,933,952,818.\n\nWait, but according to the other method, it's 2,266,398,614. So which is correct?\n\nWait a second, maybe I made an error in the breakdown method.\n\nWait, first number is 383,759,132.\n\nSecond number is 156,717,438.\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add the two numbers step by step without breaking them into parts.\n\nStarting with 785,961,835\n\nAdd 166,967,885 to it: 199,891,914 + 181,641,357 = 5,853,376,645\n\nThen add 37,140,715 (since the second number is 460,777,991, so subtract the 072 million already added: 253,810,798 - 871,885,259 = 43,172,512).\n\nWait, but maybe that's complicating. Alternatively, let's do it digit by digit again but more carefully.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion comes from the millions place. Let me check the digit-by-digit again.\n\nFirst number's digits (from right to left):\n\nPosition 7:6, 8:1, 1:3, 8:2, 8:4, 4:2, 8:6, 4:1, 5:5\n\nSecond number's digits (from right to left):\n\nPosition 5:2, 9:1, 8:3, 1:1, 4:7, 3:7, 0:1, 5:0, 7:2\n\nSo adding position by position:\n\nPosition 8: 6 +5 =9\n\nPosition 7:7+6=7\n\nPosition1:6+3=6\n\nPosition9:2+6=78 (4, carry 4)\n\nPosition4:9+5+9=65 (3, carry8)\n\nPosition8:3+7+3=83 (5, carry5)\n\nPosition1:6+2+0=78 (0, carry0)\n\nPosition2:3+8+8=48 (3, carry7)\n\nPosition8:2+7+8=45 (5, carry5)\n\nThen the final carryover 6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition02:9,\n\nPosition0:0,\n\nPosition4:5,\n\nPosition9:5,\n\nPosition8:2,\n\nPosition3:9,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition8:3,\n\nPosition1:0,\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nThus, the number is 4,603,797,379.\n\nWait, but then why does the other method give a different result?\n\nLet me recalculate the other way:\n\nFirst number: 425,960,651\n\nSecond number: 565,094,562\n\nAdding the two:\n\nLet me add 161,779,626 + 037,896,431.\n\nBreak it into:\n\n088,522,949 + 096,551,847 = 7,855,733,737\n\n52,446,798 +95,914,532 =656,876,196 → total so far 3,521,379,248\n\n5,424,684 +8,656,986 =55,787,418 → total 9,596,655,933\n\nNow the remaining parts:\n\nFirst number's remaining: 203,858\n\nSecond number's remaining: 359,946\n\nAdding those: 936,572 + 627,156.\n\nLet me compute 869,853 + 172,370.\n\n010,867 + 417,877 = 0,336,626\n\nThen add 17,759: 9,153,794 + 65,794 = 4,676,211\n\nSo total sum is 6,418,682,164 + 5,413,352 = 7,577,098,858.\n\nHmm, so now two different results: 5,168,909,552 vs. 5,063,492,371.\n\nThis suggests a miscalculation in one of the methods. Let me check with calculator-like addition:\n\nLet me write the two numbers:\n\n225,450,873\n\n+301,684,549\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 9+2=7\n\nTens:9+1=9\n\nHundreds:6+7=8\n\nThousands:5+9=22 (carryover 6)\n\nTen thousands:2+2=92 plus 5=26 (carryover9)\n\nHundred thousands:8+6=45 plus7=62 (carryover6)\n\nMillions:2+9=81 plus0=62 (carryover4)\n\nTen millions:9+7=09 plus0=08 (carryover3)\n\nHundred millions:2+4=21 plus3=88 (carryover6)\n\nBillions: carryover4.\n\nWait, wait, hold on! Here's the mistake. In the hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 4, second number's is 2. Adding them with carryover gives 8+3+7 (carryover from the previous step) =88. So we write 0 and carryover 0 to the billions place. Thus, the digits would be:\n\nBillions place: 2 (carryover), then the rest.\n\nThus the total is 8,4 (hundred millions place) but let me structure it properly.\n\nWait, let me do this step-by-step again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n2. Units: 9 +8 =6\n\n5. Tens:4+3=6\n\n6. Hundreds:4+7=0\n\n0. Thousands:8+0=00 → 4, carryover 4\n\n0. Ten thousands:9+6=38 +8=43 → 7, carryover5\n\n2. Hundred thousands:9+6=02 +4=67 →6, carryover2\n\n2. Millions:0+9=66 +0=92 →7, carryover5\n\n2. Ten millions:2+6=53 +6=71 →2, carryover9\n\n8. Hundred millions:8+0=81 +9=56 →6, carryover0\n\n71. Billions: carryover2 →0\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n2 (units), 1 (tens),3 (hundreds),0 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),8 (hundred thousands),3 (millions),1 (ten millions),0 (hundred millions),8 (billions).\n\nThus the number is written as 9,994,309,732.\n\nAh! So the error in the previous breakdown method was that when I broke it into parts, I incorrectly added the remaining parts. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst number: 066,376,624 = 164,757,773 + 96,457,329 + 7,162,979 + 709,728\n\nWait, actually, 793,096,349 is 398,488,519 + 47,796,149 + 351,324?\n\nWait, let's parse the number correctly.\n\nThe first number is 715,553,318.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- The first three digits (hundred millions, ten millions, millions): 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 7 (millions). So that's 410,786,784.\n\nThen the next three digits (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands): 0 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands). That's 900,371.\n\nThen the last three digits (hundreds, tens, units): 148.\n\nSo total is 004,467,994 + 938,195 + 482 = 550,933,856. Correct.\n\nSecond number: 995,752,449.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nFirst three digits: 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions) → 908,983,673.\n\nNext three digits: 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands) → 556,586.\n\nLast three digits: 008.\n\nThus, total is 351,719,436 + 693,693 + 151 = 516,881,452. Correct.\n\nSo when adding the two numbers:\n\n585,534,373 + 244,963,395 = 8,536,811,723.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts:\n\n822,684 + 201,767 = 8,204,333.\n\nThen 319 +907 =744.\n\nThus total sum is 6,938,578,852 + 0,447,184 = 3,484,012,714 +749 =8,714,788,302.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition, it's 5,809,953,351.\n\nThere's a 26 million difference here. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps the breakdown is wrong. Let me re-express the first number:\n\n552,608,442:\n\n8 (hundred millions) → 426,453,159\n\n1 (ten millions) → 05,419,399\n\n1 (millions) →0,649,784\n\n4 (hundred thousands) →728,525\n\n7 (ten thousands) →19,272\n\n9 (thousands) →0,535\n\n4 (hundreds) →572\n\nWait, so adding those components:\n\n284M +93M =355M,\n\n+4M →249M,\n\n+317k →091,921,276,\n\n+43k →063,978,751,\n\n+3k →765,864,433,\n\n+878 →794,508,184. Correct.\n\nSecond number: 191,127,151.\n\n559M +99M +6M =455M,\n\n+214k →381,694,324,\n\n+80k →035,486,541,\n\n+490 →854,489,374. Correct.\n\nThus adding the two numbers:\n\n583,270,773 + 800,142,396.\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n845,666,865\n\n+035,795,410\n\n= (247,647,544 + 295,164,842) + (246,381 + 666,776)\n\n= 9,603,974,871 + (9,788,881)\n\n= 3,962,652,766.\n\nBut digit by digit