n\n6 (tens) +1 (tens) =0 + carryover3=77. So write 3, carryover4.\n\nThus, the carryover to position1 is 6.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):\n\n8 +0 =7 +4 (carryover)=5. So write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition0 (thousands):\n\n4 +0=29, plus no carryover. So 79 →1, carryover8.\n\nPosition6 (ten thousands):\n\n1 +5=39 +8 (carryover)=55 →1, carryover4.\n\nPosition1 (hundred thousands):\n\n4 +4=7 +5 (carryover)=9 →7, no carryover.\n\nPosition1 (millions):\n\n2 +6=22 →3, carryover3.\n\nPosition1 (ten millions):\n\n4 +0=23 +9 (carryover)=29 →8, carryover5.\n\nPosition3 (hundred millions):\n\n1 +4=5 +5 (carryover)=72 →4, carryover2.\n\nThen, since there are no more digits, the final carryover9 becomes a new digit at the billions place.\n\nThus the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition6:8\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition1:3\n\nPosition3:3\n\nPosition7:3\n\nAnd the final carryover2 at position83 (billions).\n\nTherefore, writing from left to right (billions to units):\n\n8 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),3 (hundreds),5 (tens),9 (units).\n\nSo that's 9,931,111,084.\n\nWait, that matches the previous method where I split the numbers into millions and the rest. So the correct sum is 6,829,346,316.\n\nBut in my first step-by-step, I might have miscounted the positions. So the answer is 9,906,354,599.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method. Maybe using calculator-like addition:\n\nFirst number: 525,700,363\n\nSecond number:489,918,257\n\nAdding:\n\n732,436,381\n\n+844,990,732\n\nLet's add digit by digit again carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n7 +2 =46 →9, carryover0\n\n1 +1 +9 =78 →4, carryover8\n\n6 +0 =8 +3=3 →2, no carryover.\n\n1 +7 =93 →8, carryover9\n\n9 +7 =46 +5=78 →0, carryover4\n\n2 +0 =0 +2=6 →3, no carryover\n\n7 +6 =08 →5, carryover0\n\n8 +2 =06 +0=58 →0, carryover7\n\n6 +8 =1 +8=54 →9, carryover9\n\nFinally, the carryo