e ten thousands place. Let me check again:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number's digits:\n\nBillions: 5 (since it's a 1-digit number, so up to hundred millions)\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:5\n\nUnits:7\n\nSecond number:\n\nBillions:5\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:7\n\nUnits:1\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from units:\n\n7+9=0\n\nTens:9+7=6\n\nHundreds:1+5=6\n\nThousands:1+9=1\n\nTen thousands:1+7=48 → write 0, carryover8\n\nHundred thousands:2+2=1 +9=0\n\nMillions:4+6=7\n\nTen millions:7+9=41 → write2, carryover4\n\nHundred millions:5+1=08 +4=46 → write0, carryover6\n\nBillions:7+6=9 +6=8\n\nSo the total is 5 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),1 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),3 (hundreds),1 (tens),4 (units). So yes, that's 3,883,803,831.\n\nWait, but when I first thought I had 8,777,119,179, which is different. So I must have made a mistake in the first calculation. Let me see where.\n\nIn the first attempt, I thought:\n\nAfter the ten thousands place (70), then hundred thousands was 8+8 +4=1, but that was wrong. Wait, in the second number's hundred thousands place, it's actually 3, not 6. Because in the second number: 546,288,410. Breaking down:\n\n8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions),2 (millions),5 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands),7 (thousands), etc. So the hundred thousands digit is8, not 4. So in my first calculation I must have mistakenly taken the hundred thousands digit as 1, leading to an error. That was the mistake.\n\nSo the correct sum is indeed 7,571,453,568.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can add the numbers using another method, like splitting them into parts.\n\nLet me try adding 977,387,455 + 201,061,767.\n\nBreaking down both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 953,643,499 + 65,785,771 + 9,414,1